---
/**
 * SiteHeader — grouped primary nav (lg+). The mobile bar takes over below lg.
 *
 * Why grouped: the site outgrew a flat bar. Ten flat links leave EXACTLY 0px of
 * slack at the lg breakpoint (1024px) — measured — and visibly break there: the
 * wordmark collides with the first link and three items wrap. Grouping Bio /
 * Approach / Projects under "About" and Blog / AI News under "Writing" takes it
 * to seven roomy top-level items, and the space that frees is what lets
 * Confirmation Outlook (a hosted service with no /work/ row) and Review
 * Autopilot (the lead offer) both have a nav home. Nothing was demoted to get
 * here, and the top-level count must stay at seven.
 *
 * WORK AND SERVICES BECAME ONE SEAT ON 2026-09-11, at the operator's direction,
 * and the freed seat became "Compare". THE ROW HAS NO EIGHTH SEAT AND THAT IS
 * MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: at 1024px the container is 976px, seven seats use
 * 924px, and 52px is not enough for an eighth. A clone of an existing seat was
 * appended to the live row with six candidate labels — Reading, Compare,
 * Comparisons, What it costs, Costs, Readings — and every one of them wrapped,
 * including the five-letter one. So the three comparison sheets could only get
 * a nav home by a seat being given up, which is what happened.
 *
 * WHY THOSE TWO AND NOT ANOTHER PAIR. Work held three children that PROVE and
 * Services held two that SELL, and a visitor deciding between them was being
 * asked to know which of those two words meant "the things he makes". They are
 * one menu now, selling pages first. The merged seat keeps Work's label and
 * Work's position, because "Work" is the shorter word — which is what pays for
 * the new seat — and because D29's ordering note below still governs.
 *
 * Top-level ORDER is unchanged by D29 on purpose. Main Street is now the LOCAL
 * page — a narrower audience than before, not a wider one — so promoting it
 * above Work would bury the demo every visitor should try behind a page most
 * of them are not in the market for.
 *
 * THE CONTACT SEAT WAS GIVEN UP ON 2026-09-12 TO PAY FOR THE FULL MENU, at the
 * operator's direction, and the row is SIX seats plus a menu control. Everything
 * above about there being no eighth seat held exactly as written: the control
 * went in, `npm run measure:nav` reported WRAPPED at 1024px, and the header's own
 * rule — a top-level seat is only ever available by giving one up — was the
 * answer rather than a paragraph about it.
 *
 * THE THREE WAYS OUT WERE MEASURED, NOT ARGUED, and the numbers are the record
 * because they are what ruled the other two out. Slack in the HEADER CONTAINER
 * at 1024px, where the container is 976px:
 *
 *     seven seats, symbol-only control, button shaved to px-3      9px
 *     six seats, symbol-only control, everything else full size   64px
 *     six seats, the control carries the word "Menu"              16px   ← this
 *
 * THOSE SIXTEEN PIXELS ARE GONE AND THE ROW IS AT 9px, because the seats changed
 * again later the same day: "Compare" became the longer "Resources", About was
 * dissolved, and AI News took a seat. The reading above is kept because it is
 * what the Contact decision was made against — a superseded measurement is a
 * record of a choice, where a silently updated one is a rewrite of it. The
 * CURRENT number lives beside the Resources seat below, which is where the next
 * person changing a label will actually be reading.
 *
 * SIXTEEN PIXELS WAS NOT TIGHT BY THIS ROW'S STANDARDS. It is about what the row
 * carried before the menu existed, and the nine-pixel option bought its room by
 * narrowing the primary button and shrinking a click target, which is three
 * cosmetic compromises to buy one decision's worth of space.
 *
 * WHY CONTACT AND NOT ANOTHER SEAT. The "Start a project" button sits
 * immediately beside where that seat was and points at the same page, so the row
 * was carrying two controls for one destination — which is the duplication rule
 * this file already states about /ai-fit/. Contact is still reachable three
 * ways: that button, the full menu, and the phone bar's own tab.
 *
 * AND THE COST IS REAL, so it is written down rather than left for somebody to
 * rediscover. "Start a project" asks for more commitment than "Contact" does. A
 * visitor who only wants to ask a question may not press it, and if that turns
 * out to matter the fix is the button's LABEL rather than the seat coming back.
 *
 * Zero JS. The panels reveal on `group-hover` AND `group-focus-within`, and they
 * hide with `opacity-0 pointer-events-none` — NOT `invisible`. This is
 * load-bearing: `visibility: hidden` removes the child links from the tab order,
 * which would strand a keyboard user on a menu they can never open. Opacity keeps
 * them focusable, so tabbing into one reveals its panel.
 */
import Wordmark from './Wordmark.astro';
import NavIcon from './NavIcon.astro';
import { ChevronDown, Menu as MenuIcon, X } from '@lucide/astro';
import { dest, menu, assertSiteMap, cta, ctaHref } from '../data/site-map.js';

assertSiteMap('SiteHeader');

const path = Astro.url.pathname;

/* THE BUTTON ASKS FOR WHAT THIS PAGE'S READER IS ACTUALLY DOING, since
   2026-09-12. It said "Start a project" on every page and pointed at a bare
   /contact/ — the same question on /bio/, where somebody is deciding whether
   this is a real person, as on /quoted/, where they are pricing work.

   THE RULE LIVES IN src/data/site-map.js AND NOT HERE. Every page is already in
   that file, an assertion there refuses an intent ContactForm.jsx would not
   recognize, and putting the map here would mean the one file that knows every
   page and the one that decides what to ask for are different files.

   A PAGE NOT IN THE MAP FALLS BACK RATHER THAN THROWING. `dest()` throws on an
   unknown page because a menu row pointing nowhere is a defect; a BUTTON is
   different — every layout renders this header, including error and utility
   routes that are deliberately not destinations, and a 404 that fails to render
   because its button had no rule would be a worse failure than a generic
   button. */
const pageCta = cta(path);

type Item = { href: string; label: string; note?: string };
/* A HEADING IS A CHILD THAT IS NOT A LINK, added 2026-09-11. The first seat now
   carries ten children, which the operator declined as a flat list, so they sit
   under small headings inside the one menu. A heading is a row in `children`
   rather than a nesting level on purpose: nesting would mean a second hover
   target, and a menu you have to hover twice to read is worse than a long one.

   EVERYTHING THAT WALKS `children` MUST SKIP THESE. `groupActive` below does it
   with a type guard rather than an optional-chain, because `isActive(undefined)`
   returns false quietly — so a heading would have made the active underline
   silently unreachable for a seat rather than erroring. */
type Heading = { heading: string };
type Child = Item | Heading;
const isItem = (c: Child): c is Item => 'href' in c;
type Entry = { label: string; href?: string; children?: Child[] };

/* A CHILD IS NAMED BY ITS ADDRESS NOW, 2026-09-12, and its words come from
   src/data/site-map.js. The label and the note used to be typed here AND typed
   again in MobileTabBar.astro, where four comments promised the two were kept
   matching by hand. They were not, three times over.

   THE ARRANGEMENT STAYS HERE AND ONLY THE ARRANGEMENT. Which seat a page sits
   under, in what order, under which heading, is this surface's own judgment and
   nothing else reads it. What the page is CALLED is a fact about the page.

   SEAT LABELS ARE STILL TYPED, deliberately. "Services", "Free check", "AI Fit"
   and the rest are MEASURED against the row's width at 1024px — they are
   shortenings that fit, not names for pages, and every one has its measurement
   recorded in the comment beside it. */
const child = (href: string): Item => {
  const d = dest(href);
  return { href: d.href, label: d.label, note: d.note };
};

const nav: Entry[] = [
  {
    label: 'Services',
    href: '/services/',
    children: [
      // The most approachable demo gets a permanent nav seat without growing the top-level
      // row, which has exactly 0px of slack at 1024px (D23, measured).
      /* THE SHELF TAKES THE FIRST SEAT, 2026-08-25. Review Autopilot held it as
         the lead offer; D34 moved that to /check/ and the shelf is now what a
         local visitor is actually being sold. Inside the dropdown rather than
         at the top level, because the row has exactly 0px of slack at 1024px
         (D23, measured) and an eighth item would wrap. */
      /* THESE TWO LABELS SAY WHAT EACH PAGE IS FOR, since 2026-09-09. They read
         "Finished tools" and "All live tools" — both describing what is ON the
         page, sitting one above the other in the same dropdown, so a reader had
         no way to tell that the first SELLS and the second PROVES. One buys, one
         is evidence. The notes underneath were already doing this job alone. */
      /* THE TWO SELLING PAGES LEAD, since Work and Services merged on
         2026-09-11. They were the whole of the Services dropdown and they are
         what a visitor hovering this seat is most often after; the three below
         them are the proof. Putting the proof first would open the menu on the
         industrial work, which is the part D36 says is caliber rather than the
         offer. */
      /* ── HEADED GROUPS, 2026-09-11, AND THE SEAT IS RENAMED ────────────────
         The operator's answer in the alignment session: rename this seat so the
         top row says plainly what is for sale, hang the invisible products inside
         it, and split the result under headings rather than leaving ten flat rows.
         No seat was given up and none was added.

         WHY FIVE PAGES WERE INVISIBLE. /advisory/ and /rescue/ were each reached
         by ONE body link on /services/ and by nothing in any menu, while three
         comparison sheets that sell nothing held a whole seat; /upkeep/,
         /visibility/ and /supply-chain/ did not exist yet. A visitor could not
         find the two most expensive things on the site.

         THE LABEL IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN, and the measurement is recorded
         because the numbers are what ruled the alternatives out. At 1024px the row
         had 49px of slack carrying "Work". Re-run
         `node scripts/measure-nav.mjs --seat=<current label> "Candidate"` and read
         the HEADER CONTAINER, never the nav element:

             "Build"         51px        "What I build"   5px
             "Offer"         50px        "What I make"    2px
             "For sale"      32px        "What you buy"   WRAPS
             "Services"      26px

         FOUR FIT AND "Services" IS THE ONE, which is a judgment on top of the
         measurement rather than from it. It is the word a visitor scans for when
         they want to know what is sold; "Build" and "Offer" describe the act
         rather than the thing, and "For sale" reads like a yard sign. D99 merged
         Services away because a visitor had to know which of two words meant "the
         things he makes" — with no competing "Work" seat that confusion is gone,
         which is what makes the word available again.

         THE GROUP HEADINGS ARE THREE AND THE SPLIT IS BY COMMITMENT, not by
         subject. What ends, what recurs every month, and what is only evidence. A
         reader deciding whether to start something cares first whether it stops. */
      { heading: 'Work that ends' },
      child('/services/'),
      child('/websites/'),
      child('/tools/'),
      child('/supply-chain/'),
      child('/rescue/'),
      { heading: 'Month after month' },
      child('/advisory/'),
      child('/upkeep/'),
      child('/visibility/'),
      { heading: 'Proof, not for sale' },
      child('/work/'),
      child('/confirmation-outlook/'),
    ],
  },
  /* THE CHECK IS THE LEAD OFFER, so it gets the first seat after Work rather
     than being buried in a dropdown. The row had exactly 0px of slack at 1024px
     with ten flat links (D23, measured), which is why AI News moved down into
     About to make room rather than an eighth item being added. */
  { label: 'Free check', href: '/check/' },
  { label: 'Main Street', href: '/main-street/' },
  /* SERVICES BECAME A DROPDOWN 2026-09-03, held three children until 2026-09-06
     when the assessment took a top-level seat, and was MERGED INTO WORK on
     2026-09-11. Its two children moved up there and lead that menu. This seat is
     now the three comparison sheets, which had no nav home at all and were
     reachable only from each other.

     /ai-fit/ is NOT duplicated here, and that rule survives the merge. A link at
     the top level and again inside a dropdown is two seats for one page, and the
     dropdown copy is the one nobody needed.

     THE LABEL IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN, and the measurement is in the header
     comment: six candidates were clone-tested in the live row as an EIGHTH seat
     and all six wrapped, which is why a seat had to be given up rather than
     added. "Compare" is what these three pages do, it is one word, and it is
     shorter than the "Services" it replaces — so the row gains width rather than
     losing it. Re-run `npm run measure:nav` after any change here, and measure
     the HEADER CONTAINER rather than the nav element.

     THERE IS NO `href` ON PURPOSE. The other two grouped seats point at a page
     that summarizes their children; these three sheets have no index and writing
     one would be a fourth surface repeating what the three already say. So this
     seat opens a menu and nothing else, exactly like About. */
  /* ── RENAMED "RESOURCES" ON 2026-09-12, and the rename was forced by what
        moved in. "Compare" described three pages exactly. With the deadline list
        and the FAQ under it, that word is now describing a third of its own
        contents, and a seat label that is wrong about its own children is worse
        than a vaguer one that is right.

        WHAT THIS SEAT IS FOR: everything a reader opens while deciding, all of
        it free and none of it selling. The three comparison sheets, the Craven
        County deadline list, and the straight answers. /deadlines/ had NO seat
        at all before this and one inbound link on the whole site.

        STILL NO `href`, for the reason the Compare seat had none: these pages
        have no index, and writing one would be a fifth surface repeating what
        the four already say.

        THE LABEL IS MEASURED AND THEN JUDGED, and both halves are recorded.
        Slack left in the HEADER CONTAINER at 1024px, which is 976px inside its
        padding:

            "Resources"   9px   ← this
            "Free help"  17px
            "Answers"    21px
            "Reading"    25px
            "Guides"     32px

        "RESOURCES" IS THE TIGHTEST AND IT IS STILL THE RIGHT ONE, which is the
        judgment on top of the measurement. Every shorter candidate is wrong
        about at least one child: a platform fee comparison is not a "Guide",
        "Answers" describes the FAQ and not a list of filing dates, and
        "Reading" describes none of them. A seat label that is false about its
        own children costs more than eight pixels.

        NINE PIXELS MEANS NO LABEL ANYWHERE CAN GROW BY A LETTER. If one has to,
        a seat comes out — that is this row's only currency and always has been.

        AND THE TOOL COULD NOT MEASURE THIS SEAT UNTIL TODAY. `measure-nav.mjs`
        picked a seat's trigger with `querySelector('a')`, which searches the
        whole subtree including the open dropdown panel. A seat with no `href`
        has a <span> trigger, so it measured a panel row instead, found nothing
        swappable on it, and reported "no seat found" — a cause that was not
        true. "Compare" had the same shape from the day it was made, so no
        candidate for it was ever measurable and nothing said so. Fixed
        2026-09-12 with `:scope >`, and the two failures now name themselves
        apart. */
  {
    label: 'Resources',
    children: [
      child('/websites/compare/'),
      child('/getting-paid/'),
      child('/stores/'),
      child('/deadlines/'),
      child('/faq/'),
    ],
  },
  /* THE ASSESSMENT TOOK ITS OWN SEAT 2026-09-06, at the operator's direction,
     and FAQ moved down into About to pay for it. The top-level count did not
     move and cannot: the row has exactly 0px of slack at 1024px (D23, measured),
     so a seat is only ever available by giving one up.

     WHY THIS TRADE AND NOT ANOTHER. FAQ is the item a visitor reaches after
     deciding to look into this practice; the assessment is one they can buy.
     Since 2026-09-06 it is also the only offer whose PRICE DEPENDS on a rule
     they have to read — two forms, and the operator picks which — so burying it
     one click down asks somebody to find the explanation before they know there
     is one.

     THE LABEL IS MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN, and the full product name does not fit.
     Measured 2026-09-06 by swapping the text in a live 1024px page and reading
     the header container's remaining width, which is 976px inside its padding:

         "AI Fit"             52px slack
         "AI fit"             55px slack
         "AI Fit check"        8px slack
         "Where AI fits"      WRAPS to two rows
         "AI Fit Assessment"  WRAPS to two rows

     So "AI Fit" it is. "AI Fit check" fits and was refused anyway: /check/ is
     the FREE lead offer two seats to the left, and two nav items both ending in
     "check" is a reader working out which one costs money.

     Measure again after any change here, and measure the HEADER CONTAINER, not
     the nav element -- the nav shrink-wraps to its own content and reports 0px
     of slack at every width, which reads exactly like the row being full. */
  { label: 'AI Fit', href: '/ai-fit/' },
  /* ── THE ABOUT SEAT WAS DISSOLVED ON 2026-09-12, and this is the trade that
        paid for everything above it.

        "Resources" is two characters longer than the "Compare" it replaced, and
        AI News wanted a seat of its own. Neither fits in a row that had 16px.
        Giving up a seat is the only way this row ever finds width — the rule
        this file has stated since D23 — and About was the one that could go,
        because ALL of its children are in the full menu a control away, under a
        heading that names them better than "About" did.

        AI NEWS CAME BACK UP TO THE TOP LEVEL, which is where it was until
        2026-09-06 when it moved down to pay for the assessment's seat. It is
        the one page on this site that is different every single day, and a
        daily page buried two levels down is a daily page nobody returns to.

        AND THE COST IS NAMED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER. /bio/ no longer has
        a route from the top row, and the bio is D33's whole differentiator —
        five trades, four of them on a New Bern street, which no competitor here
        can publish. A stranger deciding whether this practice is real is the
        reader that page is for, and they now have to open the menu to find it.
        The operator was shown that cost and chose this anyway. If it bites, the
        fix is a seat for the bio rather than restoring a seat called About. */
  { label: 'AI News', href: '/ai-news/' },
];

const isActive = (href: string) =>
  path === href || path.startsWith(href.endsWith('/') ? href : href + '/');
// A group reads as active when any of its children is the current page.
const groupActive = (e: Entry) =>
  (e.href ? isActive(e.href) : false)
  || (e.children?.filter(isItem).some((c) => isActive(c.href)) ?? false);
---
{/* The frosted blur is DESKTOP-ONLY (`lg:`). A sticky header carrying a
    `backdrop-filter` sits pinned over scrolling content, so the browser
    re-rasterizes whatever is behind it on EVERY scroll frame — on a phone that is a
    top cause of choppy scrolling. On mobile it goes near-solid instead: visually
    near-identical at that size (the net barely reads behind a 95% bar anyway) and
    free to composite.

    The blur is a Tailwind UTILITY, so it CANNOT be turned off from @layer components
    — v4 puts utilities in a later layer, which wins regardless of specificity. It has
    to be made conditional here, in the markup. */}
<header
  class="sticky top-0 z-50 border-b border-line bg-canvas/95 lg:bg-canvas/80 lg:backdrop-blur-md lg:supports-[backdrop-filter]:bg-canvas/65"
>
  <div class="mx-auto flex h-16 max-w-6xl items-center justify-between px-6">
    <Wordmark />
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <nav class="hidden items-center gap-0.5 lg:flex" aria-label="Primary">
        {
          nav.map((entry) => {
            const active = groupActive(entry);
            // `relative` anchors the active underline; the link is inline-flex so the
            // chevron rides beside the label, which means the underline must be an
            // absolutely-positioned child, not a block sibling.
            const linkCls = `relative inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition-colors duration-200 ${
              active ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink-muted hover:text-ink'
            }`;
            const underline = (
              <span
                class="absolute bottom-1 left-1/2 h-px w-4 -translate-x-1/2 bg-accent"
                aria-hidden="true"
              />
            );

            if (!entry.children) {
              return (
                <a href={entry.href} aria-current={active ? 'page' : undefined} class={linkCls}>
                  {entry.label}
                  {active && underline}
                </a>
              );
            }

            return (
              <div class="group relative">
                {/* The trigger is a real link when the group has a page of its own
                    (Work → /work/); About and Writing are labels only. */}
                {entry.href ? (
                  <a href={entry.href} aria-current={active ? 'page' : undefined} class={linkCls}>
                    {entry.label}
                    <ChevronDown
                      size={13}
                      class="opacity-50 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:translate-y-0.5"
                      aria-hidden="true"
                    />
                    {active && underline}
                  </a>
                ) : (
                  <span class={`${linkCls} cursor-default`}>
                    {entry.label}
                    <ChevronDown
                      size={13}
                      class="opacity-50 transition-transform duration-200 group-hover:translate-y-0.5"
                      aria-hidden="true"
                    />
                    {active && underline}
                  </span>
                )}

                {/* opacity-0 + pointer-events-none, never `invisible` — the links
                    must stay in the tab order so focus can open the panel. */}
                <div
                  class="pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-full w-64 pt-2 opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-150 group-focus-within:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-hover:pointer-events-auto group-hover:opacity-100"
                >
                  <ul class="panel flex flex-col gap-0.5 p-2 shadow-2xl shadow-black/50">
                    {entry.children.map((c, i) => (
                      !isItem(c) ? (
                        /* A HEADING, not a link. `role="presentation"` keeps it out
                           of the list's item count for a screen reader, which would
                           otherwise announce ten items as thirteen. The top margin is
                           suppressed on the first row so the menu does not open on a
                           gap. */
                        <li role="presentation" class={i === 0 ? '' : 'mt-2'}>
                          <span class="mono-label block px-3 pb-1 pt-1 text-cyan-deep">{c.heading}</span>
                        </li>
                      ) : (
                        <li>
                          <a
                            href={c.href}
                            aria-current={isActive(c.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                            class={`block rounded-md px-3 py-2 transition-colors ${
                              isActive(c.href) ? 'bg-surface-2 text-ink' : 'text-ink-muted hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-ink'
                            }`}
                          >
                            <span class="block text-sm font-medium">{c.label}</span>
                            {c.note && <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-ink-faint">{c.note}</span>}
                          </a>
                        </li>
                      )
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            );
          })
        }
      </nav>
      {/* ── THE FULL MENU, 2026-09-12 ──────────────────────────────────────
          The phone has carried a complete menu since the tab bar was built —
          MobileTabBar's sheet, headed "Everything on this site". The desktop
          had seven seats with partial dropdowns and no full list at all, so
          /deadlines/ was reachable from no laptop menu whatsoever.

          IT SITS OUTSIDE <nav> AND THAT IS NOT A DETAIL. The seven-seat row is
          FULL: six candidate eighth labels were clone-tested in the live row at
          1024px on 2026-09-11 and every one wrapped, including a five-letter
          one. This control lives in the right-hand cluster beside the button,
          where the phone's Menu control also lives, so the measured row is
          untouched and `npm run measure:nav` still reads the same slack.

          A <details> RATHER THAN A HOVER PANEL, matching the phone. The header
          comment above warns that `visibility: hidden` strands a keyboard user
          on a menu they can never open, and a closed <details> hides its
          content the same way — but the two are not the same case. There the
          trigger was a hover with nothing focusable to press; here <summary> is
          a real, focusable control that opens the panel when activated. That is
          what a disclosure is, and it carries native semantics for a screen
          reader with no script at all.

          THE PANEL IS `absolute`, NEVER `fixed`. The header carries
          `lg:backdrop-blur-md`, and a backdrop filter makes an element the
          containing block for its `position: fixed` descendants — the same trap
          MobileTabBar records, where the phone sheet resolved against the 64px
          bar and opened invisibly. `absolute` resolves against the sticky
          header, which is what this wants anyway. */}
      <details class="group/menu hidden lg:block">
        <summary
          class="menu-summary flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-2.5 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-ink-muted transition-colors hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-ink"
          aria-label="Everything on this site"
          title="Everything on this site"
        >
          <MenuIcon size={17} class="group-open/menu:hidden" aria-hidden="true" />
          <X size={17} class="hidden group-open/menu:block" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span>Menu</span>
        </summary>

        <div
          class="absolute inset-x-0 top-full border-b border-line bg-canvas shadow-2xl shadow-black/10"
        >
          <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 pb-9 pt-7">
            <p class="mono-label text-cyan-deep">Everything on this site</p>

            {/* Home leads, above the columns rather than inside one. Its group
                carries no heading — there is no honest heading for a single
                row, and a lone link under one of the others would be filed
                somewhere it does not belong. */}
            {
              menu()
                .filter((g) => g.title === null)
                .flatMap((g) => g.items)
                .map((d) => (
                  <a
                    href={d.href}
                    aria-current={isActive(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                    class={`mt-4 flex items-baseline gap-3 border-b border-line pb-4 transition-colors ${
                      isActive(d.href) ? 'text-ink' : 'text-ink-muted hover:text-ink'
                    }`}
                  >
                    <span class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-ink">{d.label}</span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-ink-faint">{d.note}</span>
                  </a>
                ))
            }

            {/* CSS COLUMNS RATHER THAN A GRID, because the groups are seven,
                three and two rows long and a grid row is as tall as its tallest
                cell — which would open gaps of empty space under the short
                ones. Columns pack them. `break-inside-avoid` is what stops a
                group being split across a column boundary, and without it the
                heading and its rows come apart. */}
            <div class="mt-6 gap-x-10 lg:columns-3">
              {
                menu()
                  .filter((g) => g.title !== null)
                  .map((g) => (
                    <div class="mb-7 break-inside-avoid">
                      <p class="mono-label pb-2 text-cyan-deep">{g.title}</p>
                      <ul class="flex flex-col gap-0.5">
                        {g.items.map((d) => (
                          <li>
                            <a
                              href={d.href}
                              aria-current={isActive(d.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
                              class={`flex items-start gap-3 rounded-md px-2 py-1.5 transition-colors ${
                                isActive(d.href)
                                  ? 'bg-surface-2 text-ink'
                                  : 'text-ink-muted hover:bg-surface-2 hover:text-ink'
                              }`}
                            >
                              <span class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-accent-2">
                                <NavIcon name={d.icon} size={15} />
                              </span>
                              <span class="min-w-0">
                                <span class="block text-sm font-medium">{d.label}</span>
                                <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-ink-faint">{d.note}</span>
                              </span>
                            </a>
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  ))
              }
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      {
        pageCta && (
          <a href={ctaHref(pageCta)} class="btn btn-primary inline-flex px-4 py-2 text-sm">
            {pageCta.label}
          </a>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</header>
